<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mali Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mali empires</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mali Empire was a West African empire founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a> around 1235, which flourished until the fifteenth century. It controlled trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt and reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>, whose pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1324 became famous. It succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, and its decline was followed by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mali-empire.jpg" alt="Mali Empire" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Mali_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The empire began as a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> kingdom at the upper reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> region, which developed in the eleventh and twelfth centuries as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> declined. From at least the beginning of the eleventh century Mandinka kings ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of Ka-ba, today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangaba">Kangaba</a>, in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. When Ghana's control ended in the twelfth century, the province split into twelve kingdoms.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a>, a warrior-prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita dynasty</a>, led the combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Wagadou</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> city-states against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> king Soumaoro Kanté. They defeated the Sosso army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a> in about 1235, and Sundiata was declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> over the twelve kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>, an assembly of clan delegates, set out the empire's constitution. History before the thirteenth century is unclear, since Arab chroniclers and oral traditionalists give conflicting accounts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a>'s death in about 1255, rulers took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>. In 1285 Sakura, a former slave of the imperial family who had become a general, carried out a coup, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a> line returned after his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> took the throne in about 1312 and made his pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1324-1326, where his gifts and spending of gold caused significant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Ibn Battuta visited the empire in 1352, in the reign of Suleyman, who had deposed his nephew Maghan I in 1341. Suleyman's death ended the Golden Age and began a slow decline.</p><p>In 1433-1434 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> in 1465 and Timbuktu in 1468. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Macina</a> in 1477. Mali was still a sizeable state in the fifteenth century and in the early sixteenth, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> described it, but neighbours cut away at its borders from 1507. In 1599 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-iv">Mansa Mahmud IV</a> tried to take Djenne after the Moroccan conquest of Songhai and was defeated by Moroccan forces with firearms. He died around 1610, after which no single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manden</a>, and the Keitas became provincial chiefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangaba">Kangaba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The empire was a loose structure of provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states, and the farther from the capital, the weaker the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>'s direct power. Its wealth came from taxing the gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and salt that entered its borders, rather than from direct rule of the gold-producing regions. Most people were farmers. Historians disagree about the site of the capital, placing it at Niani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, elsewhere on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> or even on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>. Its spread of language, laws and customs, notably through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> traders, left political institutions with Malian structures and terms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> into the colonial period and beyond.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mali Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Mali Empire</b>, which absorbed Wagadou in the thirteenth century, repeated and expanded Ghana's trans-Saharan commercial system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam was introduced through traders: the Kano Chronicle credits Wangarawa merchants from the <b>Mali Empire</b> with bringing it to Kano under Sarki Yaji in the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire arose on the western margins of the <b>Mali Empire</b>, whose hold over the far west of the Sahel was loosening.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kaabu Empire began in the thirteenth century as a western province of the <b>Mali Empire</b>, in the region corresponding to present-day Guinea-Bissau, southern Senegal and The Gambia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam reached the court under Yaji (1349–1385), when Wangara Muslims from the <b>Mali Empire</b> arrived and supplied an imam and a judge, but it spread gradually, and many rural Hausa, the Maguzawa, kept the old religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Songhai-speaking peoples had lived on the bend of the Niger River around Gao since at least the ninth century CE, and a Songhai kingdom under the Za dynasty (or Dia dynasty) was for a time a tributary of the <b>Mali Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
